From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 13:06:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] hostapd: use the long scan timeout for every scan

wpa_driver_nl80211_scan() aborts a scan after 10 s until the driver
delivers its first NL80211_CMD_NEW_SCAN_RESULTS event, and after 30 s
from then on. The short timeout dates from drivers without a scan
completion event. cfg80211 sends NL80211_CMD_NEW_SCAN_RESULTS or
NL80211_CMD_SCAN_ABORTED for every triggered scan, so the timeout
only guards against a driver that never completes the scan. The
vendor scan path already uses 30 s for the same reason.

A mac80211 software scan of the 5 GHz band takes 11 to 12 s while
another interface on the same radio is associated, because mac80211
suspends the scan on every channel. The 10 s timeout aborts the first
scan of every interface that starts after the first association.
Network selection does not retry an aborted scan, so those interfaces
stay in SCANNING.

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
 src/drivers/driver_nl80211_scan.c |   22 +++++++++-------------
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

--- a/src/drivers/driver_nl80211_scan.c
+++ b/src/drivers/driver_nl80211_scan.c
@@ -455,19 +455,15 @@ int wpa_driver_nl80211_scan(struct i802_
 	}
 
 	drv->scan_state = SCAN_REQUESTED;
-	/* Not all drivers generate "scan completed" wireless event, so try to
-	 * read results after a timeout. */
-	timeout = drv->uses_6ghz ? 20 : 10;
-	if (drv->uses_s1g)
-		timeout += 5;
-	if (drv->scan_complete_events) {
-		/*
-		 * The driver seems to deliver events to notify when scan is
-		 * complete, so use longer timeout to avoid race conditions
-		 * with scanning and following association request.
-		 */
-		timeout = 30;
-	}
+	/*
+	 * cfg80211 sends NL80211_CMD_NEW_SCAN_RESULTS or
+	 * NL80211_CMD_SCAN_ABORTED for every triggered scan, so this timeout
+	 * only guards against a driver that never completes the scan. A
+	 * shorter first-scan timeout aborts a mac80211 software scan that
+	 * runs while another interface on the radio is associated, and
+	 * network selection does not retry an aborted scan.
+	 */
+	timeout = 30;
 	wpa_printf(MSG_DEBUG, "Scan requested (ret=%d) - scan timeout %d "
 		   "seconds", ret, timeout);
 	eloop_cancel_timeout(wpa_driver_nl80211_scan_timeout, drv, bss->ctx);
